The box-side half of the CPU-job memory cap (branch cpu-mem-cap, 2026-11-07), for the operator to apply.
Files on the box, relative to ~/b70: scripts/cpu-refs-chain.sh and logs/cpu-refs/queue.txt (made from
their 2026-11-06 copies). Apply when the chain is not running (pgrep -af cpu-refs-chain), after the
branch is merged and pulled into ~/b70/trees/cpu-refs (the chain runs its jobs there):

  cd ~/b70 && patch -p1 --dry-run < <tree>/tools/oracle/box-cpu-refs-chain-mem-cap.patch \
           && patch -p1 < <tree>/tools/oracle/box-cpu-refs-chain-mem-cap.patch

What it changes:
- cpu-refs-chain.sh: CAP_GB=56 (run_in_container.sh's default cap); a line whose peak_gb is above it is
  REFUSED unless its class is Q; class Q = validation not running and ~/b70-gpu.lock free, run with
  ORACLE_MEMORY=<peak_gb>g (run_in_container.sh then holds that lock for the run; its exit 75 = a GPU
  job took the lock first: the line goes back to queued); the RAM gate is MemAvailable >= cap + 8
  (was peak + 20); the peak column is anon + shmem + swap of the job's containers (cgroup memory.stat),
  not docker stats, which counts the active page cache of the weights a job reads.
- queue.txt: the MEM=80g overrides dropped (the jobs run under the 56g cap, their memory plans read it);
  the BF16 Bolt runs and the q4exp golden rescore / prune (96 / 90 GB) become class Q.
  q4exp-a4-ref's state is "failed rc=137": to run it again, move state/q4exp-a4-ref.env to ~/b70/trash.

